prompt.text:
Implement a new controller for a workshop-management toolbar with this exact API:

```ts
export interface Workshop { id: number; title: string; roomCode: string; }
export interface WorkshopRow { key: string; label: string; room: string; }
export interface WorkshopFilter { text: string; room: string | null; }
export type WorkshopLoader = () => Promise<readonly Workshop[]>;
export type WorkshopMapper = (workshop: Workshop) => WorkshopRow;
export type AddWorkshopDialog = () => Promise<boolean>;
export type ErrorReporter = (error: unknown) => void;

export class WorkshopToolbar {
  constructor(loader: WorkshopLoader, mapper: WorkshopMapper, dialog: AddWorkshopDialog, reportError: ErrorReporter);
  subscribe(listener: (filter: WorkshopFilter) => void): () => void;
  emitFilter(filter: WorkshopFilter): void;
  getRows(): WorkshopRow[];
  initialize(): Promise<boolean>;
  openAddDialog(): Promise<boolean>;
}
```

Listeners run synchronously in subscription order. Unsubscribing is idempotent. `emitFilter` must give every listener a distinct copy so mutation by one listener cannot affect another or the caller. `initialize` loads workshops, maps them in source order, stores copies of mapped rows, and returns `true`. On loading or mapping failure, it reports the error exactly once, leaves the previous rows unchanged, and returns `false`. `getRows` returns deep-enough copies of the flat row objects.

`openAddDialog` awaits the dialog. If it returns `false`, do nothing and return `false`. If it returns `true`, call `initialize`; after a successful initialization emit `{ text: "", room: null }` and return `true`. If initialization fails, do not emit and return `false`. If the dialog throws or rejects, report the error once, preserve state, and return `false`. Use only the standard library.

